{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788202249,"nonce":"10e6OUmSpfCgw8+iA8MqRK2FN0c=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCORRECTED — the CATEGORY argument (a state predicate is not an event tap) is\nsound and I would not touch it. But the issue's headline WORKED EXAMPLE is\narithmetically backwards, one whole paragraph argues against a mechanism that no\nlonger exists, and one proposed path shape does not parse the digest that ships.\nTwo acceptance criteria fail as written.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 1 — `food_days \u003c 3` IS LATER THAN `Alert_LowFood`, NOT EARLIER. THE\nACCEPTANCE CRITERION BUILT ON IT WILL FAIL.\n=====================================================================\n\nThe issue's argument, and DESIGN's decisions-log entry that quotes it:\n\"`Alert_LowFood` is a lagging indicator, `food_days \u003c 3` is a leading one\", and\nthe acceptance says the predicate \"halts EARLIER than the corresponding vanilla\nalert on the same run\".\n\n`RimWorld/Alert_LowFood.MapWithLowFood`, verbatim:\n\n    int freeColonistsSpawnedCount = map.mapPawns.FreeColonistsSpawnedCount;\n    if (map.resourceCounter.TotalHumanEdibleNutrition \u003c 4f * (float)freeColonistsSpawnedCount)\n\nThe alert trips at **nutrition per COLONIST \u003c 4**.\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/DigestVerb.ResourceSection` computes\n`[\"food_days\"] = needers \u003e 0 ? Math.Round(nutrition / needers, 1) : -1` where\n`needers` is colonists PLUS PRISONERS — and the file's own field docs say so\n(\"the vanilla Alert_LowFood division (human-edible nutrition / (colonists +\nprisoners))\").\n\nSo with no prisoners the two quantities are IDENTICAL and `\u003c 3` is strictly LATER\nthan `\u003c 4`. Solving `N/(c+p) \u003c 3` against `N \u003c 4c`: the predicate only wins when\n`3p \u003e c`, i.e. more than one prisoner per three colonists. On a prisoner-free M1\ncolony the proposed run halts AFTER the alert, and the acceptance's flagship\ncriterion fails on a correct implementation.\n\nWorth noting where the mod's divisor actually comes from: `Alert_LowFood`'s\nEXPLANATION text does use `FreeColonistsSpawnedCount + PrisonersOfColonyCount`.\nThe mod mirrors the alert's description; the alert's TRIGGER uses a different\ndivisor. That is a subtle enough discrepancy that it is worth a comment in\n`DigestVerb` regardless of what happens to this issue.\n\n**Three things DO genuinely make the alert late, and none of them is the\nthreshold** — use one of these as the worked example instead:\n  * `Alert_LowFood.GetReport` opens with\n    `if ((float)Find.TickManager.TicksGame \u003c 150000f) { return false; }` — the\n    alert is suppressed outright for the first 2.5 in-game days.\n  * `RimWorld/AlertsReadout` re-evaluates round robin:\n    `private const int AlertCycleLength = 24;` with\n    `for (int i = curAlertIndex; i \u003c AllAlerts.Count; i += 24)` — each alert is\n    checked once per 24 frames — and `AlertsReadoutUpdate` returns early entirely\n    while `Mathf.Max(TicksGame, Find.TutorialState.endTick) \u003c 600`.\n  * AutoRimmer adds its own lag on top: `AlertScanner.Tick` samples every\n    `Config.AlertScanFrames = 30` frames.\n\nThe 2.5-day suppression is the strongest one and it is exactly the \"leading vs\nlagging\" story the issue wants: on day 1 the alert CANNOT fire no matter how\nempty the larder is, and a state predicate can.\n\n(DESIGN's decisions log carries the `food_days \u003c 3` framing verbatim. It should\nbe amended alongside this issue, or the next reader inherits the error from the\ndesign document rather than from the ticket.)\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 2 — THE COST ARGUMENT AIMS AT A LOOP THAT WAS DELETED\n=====================================================================\n\n\"Cadence is a knob with a sane default; a predicate is not free and the tick\nloop's budget is the whole point of 1.3.\"\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/Config.cs` says, in its own words: \"`advanceBudgetMs` was 1.3's\nper-frame DoSingleTick budget and is GONE: 1.8 sets the game's own time speed and\nlets RimWorld tick, so there is no budget to spend.\" 1.8 landed on 2026-08-31\n(`fa11564`, git-bug `b8785e8`, closed); this issue was filed 2026-08-30 19:19.\n\nSo the paragraph is not wrong about cost mattering — it is wrong about what the\ncost is measured against, and the acceptance line \"evaluation cost measured\nagainst the advance budget\" has no denominator any more. Restate both against a\nPER-FRAME cost. (The measurement precedent the acceptance cites is real —\n`RUNLOG.md` records \"overhead 0.0039 ms/frame measured through AnalyzerBridge\ndriving DPA\" — and DPA plus AnalyzerBridge are on the bench, so the method\nsurvives; only the unit changes.)\n\n**And the good news the issue does not know: `TimeDriver.Step` ALREADY DOES\nPER-FRAME STATE-PREDICATE HALTING.** It polls three live facts every frame and\nhalts on them — `if (stack != null \u0026\u0026 stack.WindowsForcePause) { Halt(\"dialog\", ...); }`,\n`if (cur == TimeSpeed.Paused) { ... Finish(\"stalled\"); }`, and the `stallFrames`\nwatchdog. A `condition` matcher is an addition to an existing poll site, not a new\ncategory of machinery. That materially shrinks the spec and it is the single most\nuseful thing to add to it.\n\nThe natural cadence unit is therefore FRAMES, counted the way `AlertScanner.Tick`\nalready counts them (`if (++frameCounter \u003c Config.AlertScanFrames) return;`), not\nticks. That is my inference, not a reading.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 3 — `digest.colonists[*].mood_pct` IS NOT A PATH INTO WHAT THE DIGEST\nEMITS\n=====================================================================\n\n`DigestVerb.ColonistSection` returns\n`new Dictionary\u003cstring, object\u003e { [\"list\"] = list, [\"total\"] = ..., [\"more\"] = ..., [\"order\"] = \"attention-desc\" }`.\nThe addressable path is `colonists.list[*].mood_pct`. This is not pedantry: the\nissue proposes \"`any`/`all` over list-valued sections\", and **no digest section is\nlist-valued at the top level** — every one of them is `{list, total, more, ...}`.\nA path grammar written to the issue's example will not parse the digest that\nships. (`resources.food_days` and `power.gain_w` are both CONFIRMED correct as\nwritten.)\n\n=====================================================================\nCONFIRMED\n=====================================================================\n\n- DESIGN.md at \"Initial import\" really did advertise\n  `until:{letter|alert|event-match|condition}`, and `TimeDriver` really shipped\n  `private enum Until { None, Ticks, Letter, Threat, Alert, Event }`. `condition`\n  never existed; `threat` was undocumented. CONFIRMED, and both are now corrected\n  in DESIGN.\n- \"Every shipped matcher is a journal tap over discrete events\" — CONFIRMED for\n  the four `until:` matchers (`Journal.OnEvent += Notice;`). Note the driver ALSO\n  has `Journal.OnRedError += NoticeRedError;`, a second tap deliberately upstream\n  of the journal's dedupe cap. So the driver already has precedent for a halt\n  source that is not an `OnEvent` journal event — another reason `condition` is\n  less exotic than the issue makes it sound.\n- \"Nothing is emitted when a continuous value crosses a threshold\" — CONFIRMED.\n  `AlertScanner.Tick` is the only producer of `alert_on`, and it emits by DIFFING\n  the readout's own `activeAlerts` list. There is no threshold-crossing emitter\n  anywhere.\n- Main-thread constraint — CONFIRMED and worth stating in the spec WHY:\n  `TimeDriver.Notice` is documented \"Any thread, called synchronously from\n  Journal.Emit\", so a predicate cannot live there. `Step()` is the only legal\n  site.\n- The three lazy-getter exemplars all check out. `MapPawns.FreeHumanlikesSpawnedOfFaction`\n  clears and refills the same list instance per access; `DangerWatcher.DangerRating`'s\n  getter writes `dangerRatingInt`/`lastUpdateTick` behind a 101-tick check and\n  its `CalculateDangerRating` re-enters `FreeColonistsSpawned`; `Room.Role` is\n  `get { if (statsAndRoleDirty) { UpdateRoomStatsAndRole(); } return role; }` and\n  that method scores EVERY `RoomStatDef` and EVERY `RoomRoleDef`. CONFIRMED.\n- \"Alerts fire late\" is real, and `Alert_TatteredApparel` is the right exemplar:\n  it is `protected override ThoughtDef Thought =\u003e ThoughtDefOf.ApparelDamaged;`\n  and `Alert_Thought.AffectedPawns` selects on the thought ALREADY EXISTING\n  (`item.needs.mood.thoughts.GetMoodThoughtsFor(Thought, tmpThoughts)`). The mood\n  penalty is the alert's precondition. CONFIRMED.\n- The halt-reporting mechanism exists: `Halt(string reason, Dictionary evt, long seq)`\n  and `data[\"halted_on\"]`/`data[\"halted_seq\"]`. CONFIRMED — with the note that\n  `halted_seq` is a JOURNAL sequence and is meaningless for a state halt.\n  `Interrupt()` already sets the precedent by calling `Halt(\"interrupted\", null, 0)`.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHREE THINGS THE ISSUE MISSES\n=====================================================================\n\n1. **`food_days` HAS A 204-TICK QUANTUM, WHICH BOUNDS THE HEADLINE ACCEPTANCE\n   CRITERION.** `RimWorld/ResourceCounter.ResourceCounterTick` is\n   `if (Find.TickManager.TicksGame % 204 == 0) { UpdateResourceCounts(); }`, and\n   `TotalHumanEdibleNutrition` reads the dictionary that tick fills. So a cadence\n   finer than 204 ticks buys nothing for any `resources.*` path, and \"halts within\n   one cadence window of the crossing\" is unachievable below ~204 ticks NO MATTER\n   what the cadence knob says. State the floor in the spec.\n2. **READ COST IS NOT UNIFORM, AND THE ISSUE PRICES \"A PREDICATE\" AS ONE THING.**\n   `resources.food_days` walks the counted-amounts dictionary calling\n   `GetStatValueAbstract(StatDefOf.Nutrition)` per def; `power.gain_w` walks every\n   net and every `powerComps` entry; `threats.danger` costs a\n   `CalculateDangerRating` at most once per 101 ticks; anything under\n   `colonists.list[*]` costs a `Room.Role` per colonist. A per-path cost note\n   belongs in the spec, and it is also the argument for restricting the initial\n   surface.\n3. **YOU CANNOT CROSS-CHECK A PREDICATE HALT FROM INSIDE THE ADVANCE.**\n   `AgentGameComponent.DrainCommands` fails every non-`pause` command with\n   `Err.Busy` while an advance is in flight. So \"halts within one cadence window\n   of the crossing\" can ONLY be checked after the halt, against `halted_on` —\n   which makes the payload the issue asks for the sole evidence the test can have,\n   not a nicety.\n\n=====================================================================\nACCEPTANCE — ITEM BY ITEM\n=====================================================================\n\n- \"halts within one cadence window of the crossing, and reports the path and\n  tripping value\" — testable only once the 204-tick floor is written in (miss 1),\n  and only observable post-hoc (miss 3). Needs the bench.\n- \"It halts EARLIER than the corresponding vanilla alert on the same run\" —\n  **WILL FAIL as written** (Correction 1), and is additionally impossible to arm:\n  `TimeDriver.Start` accepts exactly ONE `until` key (the parse is a\n  `ContainsKey` else-if chain), so a single run cannot arm `condition` and `alert`\n  simultaneously. Either the spec changes that, or the criterion becomes two runs\n  on the same fixture and seed.\n- \"A predicate that is never true exits on `timeout_ticks`, not by hanging\" —\n  testable; the machinery is already there\n  (`timeoutTicks = args.Int(\"timeout_ticks\", until == Until.Ticks ? 0 : 600000)`\n  and `if (timeoutTicks \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 TicksDone \u003e= timeoutTicks) { Finish(\"timeout\"); }`).\n- \"Evaluation cost measured against the advance budget\" — the budget does not\n  exist (Correction 2). Restate as ms/frame.\n- \"no mutation observable across an advance that only evaluates\" — **UNTESTABLE\n  AS WRITTEN, and false for two of the proposed paths.** `threats.danger` writes\n  `dangerRatingInt`/`lastUpdateTick`; `colonists.list[*].room` writes `role`,\n  `stats` and clears `statsAndRoleDirty`. Both are idempotent, RNG-free and\n  unscribed, so they are legal under the project's observer rule — but \"no\n  mutation observable\" is not the rule the project actually holds, and this\n  criterion states it in a form that either forbids two useful paths or is\n  vacuous. Restate it as the project's real invariant: nothing SCRIBED changes,\n  and the shared `Rand` stream is untouched.\n- \"Zero red errors\" — testable.\n\n=====================================================================\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n=====================================================================\n\n- That frames are the right cadence unit post-1.8. That follows from there being\n  no tick loop left, but it is a design call.\n- That the 2.5-day suppression is the best replacement worked example. It is the\n  cleanest one I found; there may be a better path (`power.gain_w` crossing zero\n  before `Alert_...` fires would also serve, and has no divisor to get wrong).","files":null}]}